<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Your phone's contacts list is a graveyard: three hundred names, most of
    them dead weight, all of them presented as equals. Nothing in it
    distinguishes the person you'd want at your funeral from a plumber you
    used once in 2019.
  </p>
  <p>
    A circle is that distinction, made explicit — and it turns out to be the
    difference between a directory and something that can actually help you
    keep the relationships you care about.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you get</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The people who matter, first") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The page opens on your actual circle, not the full roster. The
        roster still exists for when you need the plumber; it just isn't
        what greets you.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "One line of signal each") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        When you last spoke, what's coming up for them, what you meant to
        follow up on. Enough to know who needs you this week without opening
        anything.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Groups as first-class things") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The choir, the team, the parents from school, the mutual aid group.
        Most real social life happens in groups, and software that only
        modelled one-to-one friendships would miss most of it.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Actions that end in contact") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Every button here leads somewhere real — a message drafted, a date
        proposed, a visit logged. Nothing leads to a more complete profile,
        because that isn't a relationship.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Logging takes seconds</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Recording that you saw someone is meant to cost about four seconds,
    because anything more expensive doesn't get done and the whole thing
    silently stops being true. The app does the filing; you do the seeing.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The drift, named gently</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The friendship that's gone eleven months without either of you deciding
    anything is the thing this exists to catch. It's surfaced with something
    you could actually send — not as a guilt counter, and not as a metric
    you're failing.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Private, obviously</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    What you've recorded about a relationship is nobody's business,
    including the rest of your household's. The dinner can be on a shared
    calendar without your notes about the guest going anywhere.
  </p>
</section>
